2.1.6. Reward Structure
Reward-shaping plays a critical role in solving RL problems. The two reward structures implemented for this project were dense (heterogeneous) and sparse (homogeneous) rewards, shown in Figure 4. With the standard sparse reward scheme, the agent received a reward of −1 for all states except the final placement state. The agent had difficulty solving complex RL problems with this reward scheme due to the Monte Carlo (random) nature of this approach. The dense reward function implemented for this project was the heterogeneous reinforcement function [41]. In this approach, the reward improved as the agent executes the task correctly. For example, for reach, the reward improves proportionally to the distance between the EE and target sphere.


